The files in the folder "copper-tungsten-system" are the most relevant, ignore everything else as they were used to 
learn MOOSE/TMAP8 and/or adapt verification cases, which may use irrelevant parameters.

You will find the following in copper-tungsten-system:
    Cu-W    
        folder for a bilayer system where Cu faces the H source and the H recombine and diffuse out of W
        includes trapping and no trapping case
    Cu-W-Ar
        folder for a trilayer system with Cu-W-Ar, no trapping, where H goes from Cu to Ar. 
        We ended up removing argon from consideration because we realized analyzing permeation flux
        from the W was sufficient.
    individual-layers-no-trapping
        folder that includes only W and Cu, no trapping, and diffusion front checks
    val-CVD-W-Cu
        folder that compares against the CVD experiments run in Sun 2025. This includes three input files: one with only W, 
        one with only Cu, and one with the two-layer system with W on the H source side. 
        In the .params file you can see all the temperatures for which each case is run. 
        The comparison file compares the experimental permeation curves to the simulated ones.
    val-W-Eurofer
        folder for an abandoned validation case as the experiment mainly reflects the impact of changing surface kinetics
        rather than pressure as was initially believed.
    W-Cu
        folder that models the bilayer system with W facing the source. It includes the no trapping case,
        as well as various trapped cases (1 in Cu, 1 in W, 2 in W, and 3 in the whole system)
    W-Cu-W
        folder that models a tungsten-copper-tungsten system as expected to reflect the experimental case.
        The comparison.py file was added last minute and may not be flawless, though it will have to be changed anyways
        to compare it to the experimental data. Val-CVD-W-Cu may have some reusable code (ie sorting by temperature) that would make this faster.
    Cu_lam_fit.py  
        used to fit the temperature-dependent lattice constant for copper

## EXTRA NOTES

# RUN_ID
For easier comparison between simulation runs where different numerical or physical parameters may have changed, I made the simulation files output with a RUN_ID suffix, which you specify when running the code, i.e.: 
tmap8-opt -i filename.i RUN_ID = run01
In the comparison files, in the ‘legend’ array at the top, you can then specify what each run corresponds to. This method works even if you aren’t comparing anything; it’d just plot one run. Conversely, in val-CVD-W-Cu, the files are organized based on temperature, which may be more applicable when comparing to experimental data.
# CONVERGENCE ISSUES
Most convergence issues can be fixed solely by loosening nl_rel_tol and nl_abs_tol or making dtmax smaller, so if you run into failing solves,
I’d suggest tuning those first. A smaller dtmax is typically need at higher temperatures. Shrinking dtmax also improves the conservation of mass.
# STEADY STATE DETECTION
The simulation automatically ends when steady state is detected.


Lastly, I tried to make the plotting as generalizable as possible, but there may be cases where I manually entered the axes so those may need to be tweaked.



 
